# Porto Features - Cursor Rules

## Project Purpose

Shared BDD contracts (Gherkin) and fixtures aligned with [porto-data](https://github.com/gruncellka/porto-data). This package ships the contract only.

## Structure

```
porto_features/                     # Published package content
├── errors.json                     # PORTO_* catalog
├── features/
│   ├── sdk/
│   │   ├── core/                   # @sdk @core
│   │   └── providers/{id}/         # @sdk @provider:{id}
│   └── adapters/{id}/{integration}/
│       ├── marks.feature           # @adapters @provider:{id} @wire:{id}
│       └── errors.feature          # @error + unique @scenario:
└── fixtures/                       # Test data
    └── addresses/
```

## Gherkin Format

Every Feature declares exactly one of `@sdk` or `@adapters`. Paid purchase uses `When I create a mark`. Do not add fake simulate / stamp-generation features — offline resolve is `When I resolve the letter`. See `docs/vocabulary.md` and `docs/scenarios.md`.

## Key Principles

- **Implementation-agnostic**: Features describe behavior, not runner internals
- **Data-driven**: Use fixtures for test data
- **BDD**: Business-readable scenarios
- **Package-first**: Keep data under `porto_features/` so npm and PyPI ship identical files

## Adapter Examples

Do not hand-edit generated Example tables on adapter Features.

## Do NOT

- Add step definitions or runner glue here
- Reference runner internals in feature files
- Duplicate fixtures that exist in porto-data
- Ship `contracts/` or `porto_features/matrix/` in this package
- Add `mark_simulate` / `stamp_generation` / CLI stamp-simulate scenarios
- Use `@operator:` (use `@provider:{id}`) or address field `city` (use `locality`)
